\question{7}{8} The following table shows the number of books of $3$ different genres.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Genres} & \textbf{Number of books} \\
\hline
Novels & $1560$ \\
Magazines & $7020$ \\
Reference & $3180$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{subparts}
  \item If there are $8$ libraries in Hong Kong, can all the books be distributed evenly to all the libraries? Explain your answer.
  \item In the libraries, only books of the same genre can be placed on the same bookshelf and each bookshelf has the same number of books.
  \begin{subpartsii}
    \item What is the maximum number of books placed on the same bookshelf? Explain your answer.
    \item If each bookshelf contains the number of books in (b)(i), how many bookshelves are needed?
  \end{subpartsii}
\end{subparts}

\answerlines{16}

\question{8}{8} On a certain day, the temperature at $1$ p.m.\ is $22^\circ\text{C}$. The temperature changes after each hour from $1$ p.m.\ to $4$ p.m.\ are recorded as follows: (A decrease in temperature is represented by a negative number.)

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Time} & $2$ p.m. & $3$ p.m. & $4$ p.m. \\
\hline
\textbf{Temperature change ($^\circ\text{C}$)} & $+2.1$ & $-0.7$ & $-1.4$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{subparts}
  \item Find the temperatures at
  \begin{subpartsii}
    \item $2$ p.m.,
    \item $3$ p.m.,
    \item $4$ p.m.
  \end{subpartsii}
  \item If the temperature at $6$ p.m.\ is $20.8^\circ\text{C}$ and the temperature changes from $4$ p.m.\ to $5$ p.m.\ and $5$ p.m.\ to $6$ p.m.\ are the same, Candy claims that the temperature at $5$ p.m.\ is higher than the temperature at $1$ p.m. Do you agree? Explain your answer.
\end{subparts}

\answerlines{16}

